        "sourceMarkdown": "# Day 3, Week 3\n\n## Day Metadata\n- Level: K3\n- Unit Number: 1\n- Unit Theme: A Day on the Farm\n- Day: 3\n- Week: 3\n- Day Type: Power Up\n- Power Up Lesson: PU L11\n- Lesson Role: A1 Movers preparation\n\n## Circle Time\n### Topic\nTBD (Power Up theme: A Day on the Farm)\n\n### Song\nTBD\n\n### Teacher Notes\nUse Circle Time to warm up the lesson theme. Keep it oral, visual, and routine-based. Model any target sentence as a whole chunk the children can repeat.\n\n## CLIL Class\n### Lesson Outcome\nwill have practised answering basic personal questions and talking about the differences between two pictures (Al Movers Speaking Part 1).\n\n### Source\n- Teacher's Book: TB p16\n- Pupil's Book: PB p16\n- Activity Book: AB p16\n\n### New Keywords\n- farmer\n- have a picnic\n- sandwich(es)\n\n### Recycled Keywords\n- countryside\n- food and drink\n- tap\n- tree\n- wash (her) hands/boots\n- Here...\n\n### Target Sentences\n- have got\n- There is/are\n\n### Activities and Games\n1. TBD\n2. TBD\n3. TBD\n\n### Teacher / Design Guidance\n**Power Up teaching focus**\n\nThe original Power Up lesson is the source of truth for this day. The current page shows the auto-classified keywords and target sentences; the teacher can model them as words and as sentence chunks without inventing new content.\n\n## Phonics\n### Content\nTBD\n\n### Teacher Notes\nTBD\n\n## Story\n### Book / Story\nTBD\n\n### Teacher Notes\nTBD\n\n## Review Issues\n### Needs Extension\n- Umbrella item: animals\n  - Original source: Recycled Language\n  - Suggested concrete words from current scaffold: bird, cat, animals, rabbit, animal, cow, sheep, monster, dog, elephant, flamingo, frog, toad, duck, horse, mouse, bear, crocodile, monkey, snake, tiger, lion\n  - Decision needed: choose whether to teach this as a category word or replace it with concrete child-facing items.\n\n- Umbrella item: prepositions of place\n  - Original source: Recycled Language\n  - Suggested concrete words from current scaffold: under, in, next to, on, behind, between, in front of\n  - Decision needed: choose whether to teach this as a category word or replace it with concrete child-facing items.\n\n- Umbrella item: present continuous\n  - Original source: Recycled Language\n  - Suggested concrete words: TBD (no children found in earlier K lessons)\n  - Decision needed: choose whether to teach this as a category word or replace it with concrete child-facing items.\n\n### Source Issues\n- None for this day.\n",
